<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n2212" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">αἰγῠπιός</span></b> , ὁ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>vuliure</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">αἰ. γαμψώνυχες ἀγκυλοχεῖλαι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Il.
					16.428
				</span></i>
			, cf. <i><span class="en">
				17.460
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Od.
				16.217
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Hes.
				<u>Sc.</u>
				405
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Hdt.
				3.76
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>Aj.</u>
				169
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Arist.
				<u>HA</u>
				610a
			</span></i>I, etc.:—
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">αἰγυπιοὶ γῦπές τε</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Nic.
					<u>Th.</u>
					406
				</span></i>
			, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Ael.
				<u>NA</u>
				2.46
			</span></i>. (Both words seem to be generic terms, but <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">αἰ</span> is an older word chiefly found in
			poetry.)</div>
